{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788122927,"nonce":"Hb4uLc2dfLFZf8jUasbPaGApXZM=","message":"Open-question resolutions (worker, in-session on BORGES):\n\n**until-surface v1: journal-event matchers only, no predicate language.** `until ::= {letter: true|\"LetterDef\"} | {threat: true} | {alert: true|\"AlertClassName\"} | {event: {type, contains?}}`. `threat` = letter def ThreatBig|ThreatSmall. The matchers hang on a synchronous tap on Journal.Emit — every hook that writes the journal is thereby a watchpoint source for free, on the emitting thread, with the event's own seq. `event.contains` does a case-insensitive substring over string payload values; anything richer waits for demonstrated need (the spec's own deferral).\n\n**Modal windows that pause: the driver ignores ForcePaused and owns CurTimeSpeed.** The loop drives `DoSingleTick()` directly, so `WindowsForcePause` (letter dialogs, game-over) never stalls an advance; a pauseMode letter's `Pause()` call is a no-op into our already-Paused state. If anything else changes the speed mid-advance, the driver re-pins Paused next frame and reports `repinned_speed_changes` — an unpaused speed beside the loop would double-tick via TickManagerUpdate. One hazard found in the decompile and guarded: the CurTimeSpeed setter silently no-ops when `PlayerCanControl` is false (cutscene, landing confirmation), so `advance` verifies the initial pause took and fails fast with per-verb code `cannot-pause` instead of double-ticking.\n\n**Vanilla speeds: `unpause` only, for live human watching; `advance` never touches them.** The budget loop out-runs Ultrafast at half the frame rate (0.1 tps table), is immune to the TimeSlower clamp (spans reported, per the amendment's neither-honor-nor-ignore ruling), and isn't subject to the 45.45ms vanilla frame break. `unpause` reports whether the setter took (same PlayerCanControl no-op).\n\nDecisions in the same spirit:\n\n- **One long-running op at a time.** While an advance is in flight every other main-thread verb answers `busy` (with progress in the detail); `pause` is the exception — the brake pedal, interrupting the advance (its result says `reason:interrupted`, pause's own result says `was_advancing:true`). Off-thread verbs (status/version/journal) stay live throughout — that is what they are for.\n- **Deferred results.** The advance handler returns a sentinel; TimeDriver owns the command's single result and writes it at halt — on timeout and on exception too, always with the game paused (the loop never unpauses, and teardown re-Pauses defensively). The one-result invariant moves with the ownership. A `kill -9` mid-advance leaves that command unresulted by construction — the CLI's cue is the sid change in status.json; flagged for 1.4 rather than papered over here.\n- **Quota semantics.** max_tps is enforced as per-wall-second buckets (never more than the quota in any bucket). Measured avg_tps over short windows can read a couple percent above the quota purely from partial-bucket division at the edges (6000 ticks at quota 100 measured 102.1 over 58.7s); over the 5-minute acceptance window the artifact is under half a percent. Duty cycle — the thermal point of the cap — is bucket-exact.\n- **Thermal governor: file-driven, platform-honest.** Sensor resolution order: config `thermalPath` -\u003e Linux hwmon scan (k10temp/coretemp preferred — dorian's bench, where the 93C plateau lives) -\u003e `\u003croot\u003e/thermal.txt` (plain degrees C, for a sidecar to write). On BORGES, WMI MSAcpi_ThermalZoneTemperature answers \"not supported\" (probed) and Mono has no usable WMI anyway, so the governor is INERT here and says so by omitting the `thermal` status block. The machine-independent protection is the quota cap; the governor is defense-in-depth where sensors exist. Thresholds (93/90/30s) and budget (25ms, clamped) all in config.json.","files":null}]}